# the x86-64 rows written in a short form once their registers are allocated
#
# the arithmetic and test rows of a full immediate have a form one byte
# shorter for the accumulator, al, ax, eax or rax, which masc's reader takes
# for the text of the general row whenever its register is the accumulator,
# and an exchange of a register with the accumulator reads back to 90+r. each
# general row selection writes is paired with its accumulator form, the
# exchange's two forms in the order masc's rows put them, which is the order
# its reader tries them in, so the one the view admits first is taken. an
# immediate of a sign extended byte keeps its own form, which the reader
# prefers to the accumulator's.
#
# a move of zero into a general register is written as the register xored
# with itself, two bytes where b8 takes five, and a compare of a register with
# zero as its test against itself, which leaves every flag cmp does but the
# auxiliary carry, which it leaves unspecified, wherever what the two leave
# unlike each other is dead (`mirl.machine.zero`). the move is the 32-bit one,
# whose write clears the bits above, as the xor's does. a load of a float or
# vector zero from read-only data, movd, movq and movss clearing the bits of
# the xmm register above the ones they load and movups loading it whole, is
# the register xored with itself by xorps, which writes no flag and is
# baseline sse.
#
# a copy of a general register and the add or subtract after it into the
# copy, the immediate or the register they add a 32-bit one or a 64-bit one,
# is one lea of the copy's source plus the other operand, the address naming
# the 64-bit registers of the same numbers, wherever the flags the add writes
# and lea does not are dead (`mirl.machine.fuse`). lea writes the low bits the
# add does and the 32-bit one clears the bits above as the add does, and its
# address computes at 64 bits, so the copy is at least as wide as the add.
#
# and, or and xor leave every flag test of their result against itself
# defines as test does, the carry and overflow clear and the sign, zero and
# parity of the result, and add and subtract leave the sign, zero and parity
# so, which drops the test after them wherever the flags they leave unlike it
# are dead (`mirl.machine.fuse`).

